\lispPackageChapter{coingecko}

The \texttt{coingecko} package is the one you \texttt{use} from application
code.  It re-exports every external symbol of
\texttt{coingecko/rest-api} and adds the helpers I call from Limbic:
current quotes, price histories as Common Lisp universal times, and the
old Zapper-era names such as \texttt{btc/usd} and \texttt{xag/usd}.

\section{Time}

CoinGecko chart points are UNIX milliseconds.  Common Lisp wants
universal time.  These four functions sit between the two.

\lispParameterSubsection{unix-epoch}

Universal time of the UNIX epoch, 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z.

\lispFunctionSubsection{unix->universal}

Convert a UNIX timestamp in seconds or milliseconds to universal time.
Values above $10^{11}$ are treated as milliseconds, which is what
CoinGecko chart points use.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(unix->universal \lispVar{unix})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(unix->universal 0)              ; => unix-epoch
(unix->universal 1577836800)     ; => 2020-01-01T00:00:00Z
(unix->universal 1577836800000)  ; => same instant, from milliseconds
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{universal->unix}

Convert a Common Lisp universal time to UNIX seconds.

\lispFunctionSubsection{canonicalize-date}

CoinGecko \texttt{/history} dates are \texttt{YYYY-MM-DD}.  This accepts
a string (passed through), a universal time, or a
\texttt{(year month day)} list.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(canonicalize-date \lispVar{date})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(canonicalize-date "2024-01-15")   ; => "2024-01-15"
(canonicalize-date '(2024 7 4))    ; => "2024-07-04"
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{canonicalize-unix}

Turn a value into a UNIX-seconds string for range endpoints.  Integers
greater than $10^{11}$ are treated as milliseconds; integers greater
than $3 \cdot 10^{9}$ are treated as universal times; everything else is
passed through as UNIX seconds.

\section{Current Quotes}

\lispFunctionSubsection{quotes}

One cached \texttt{/simple/price} call covering the usual Limbic quote
set: Bitcoin and Ether versus \texttt{usd}, \texttt{xag}, and
\texttt{xau}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(quotes \&key (\lispVar{ids} '(:bitcoin :ethereum))
  (\lispVar{vs-currencies} '(:usd :xag :xau)))}

\lispFunctionSubsection{price}

Current price of a coin in a vs-currency.  The id may be \texttt{:btc}
or \texttt{"bitcoin"}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(price \lispVar{id} \&optional (\lispVar{vs-currency} :usd))}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(price :bitcoin :usd)
(price :eth :usd)
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{btc/usd}

The value of Bitcoin in US Dollars.

\lispFunctionSubsection{eth/usd}

The value of Ether in US Dollars.

\lispFunctionSubsection{btc/xag}

The value of Bitcoin in troy ounces of silver.

\lispFunctionSubsection{btc/xau}

The value of Bitcoin in troy ounces of gold.

\lispFunctionSubsection{eth/xag}

The value of Ether in troy ounces of silver.

\lispFunctionSubsection{eth/xau}

The value of Ether in troy ounces of gold.

\lispFunctionSubsection{xag/usd}

The value of one troy ounce of silver in US Dollars, derived as
\texttt{(btc/usd) / (btc/xag)}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{xau/usd}

The value of one troy ounce of gold in US Dollars, derived as
\texttt{(btc/usd) / (btc/xau)}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{/btc}

The value of \lispVar{id} expressed in Bitcoin, via US Dollars.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(/btc \lispVar{id})}

\lispFunctionSubsection{/eth}

The value of \lispVar{id} expressed in Ether.

\lispFunctionSubsection{/xag}

The value of \lispVar{id} expressed in troy ounces of silver.

\lispFunctionSubsection{/xau}

The value of \lispVar{id} expressed in troy ounces of gold.

\lispFunctionSubsection{btc/eth}

The value of Bitcoin expressed in Ether.

\lispFunctionSubsection{eth/btc}

The value of Ether expressed in Bitcoin.

\lispFunctionSubsection{fiat-rate}

USD-per-unit for a vs-currency (\texttt{:usd}, \texttt{:xag},
\texttt{:xau}) or a coin (\texttt{:btc}, \texttt{:eth}).  The name is
the Zapper-era one, so Limbic call sites can switch over without
renaming.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(fiat-rate \lispVar{symbol})}

\lispFunctionSubsection{1/fiat-rate}

Units per US Dollar, the inverse of \texttt{fiat-rate}.

\section{Price Histories}

This is the main Limbic entry point: Bitcoin (and other) histories as
lists of Common Lisp times and prices, rather than raw CoinGecko
payloads.

\lispFunctionSubsection{market-chart}

Raw \texttt{/coins/\{id\}/market\_chart} payload: a hash table with
\texttt{"prices"}, \texttt{"market\_caps"}, and
\texttt{"total\_volumes"}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(market-chart \lispVar{id} \&key (\lispVar{vs-currency} :usd)
  (\lispVar{days} 30) \lispVar{interval} \lispVar{precision})}

\lispFunctionSubsection{market-chart-range}

Raw \texttt{/coins/\{id\}/market\_chart/range} payload.
\lispVar{from} and \lispVar{to} may be universal times, UNIX seconds,
or UNIX milliseconds.

\lispFunctionSubsection{ohlc}

Raw \texttt{/coins/\{id\}/ohlc} payload: a vector of
\texttt{[timestamp, open, high, low, close]}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{ohlc-range}

Raw \texttt{/coins/\{id\}/ohlc/range} payload.  Analyst plan and above.

\lispFunctionSubsection{history}

Snapshot of a coin at 00:00 \textsc{Utc} on \lispVar{date}.  The date
may be \texttt{YYYY-MM-DD}, a universal time, or
\texttt{(year month day)}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(history \lispVar{id} \lispVar{date} \&key \lispVar{localization})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(history :bitcoin "2024-01-15")
(history :bitcoin '(2024 1 15))
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{price-history}

A list of \texttt{(universal-time price)} points for \lispVar{id}.
This is the function I reach for when I want Bitcoin's history.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(price-history \lispVar{id} \&key (\lispVar{vs-currency} :usd)
  (\lispVar{days} 30) \lispVar{interval})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(price-history :bitcoin :vs-currency :usd :days 365)
(price-history :btc :days 90 :interval :daily)
\end{lstlisting}

\lispReturns

A list of two-element lists.  The first element is a universal time;
the second is the price in \lispVar{vs-currency}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{ohlc-history}

A list of \texttt{(universal-time open high low close)} candles for
\lispVar{id}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(ohlc-history \lispVar{id} \&key (\lispVar{vs-currency} :usd)
  (\lispVar{days} 30) \lispVar{interval})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(ohlc-history :bitcoin :days 30)
\end{lstlisting}
